  "summary_text": "Kyle,\n\nPort Washington is running hot and pulling ahead\u2014up 8.7% on a DOW-adjusted basis month-to-date, appointments up 8%, and year-over-year you're at +45% revenue and +45% appointments. That's the story worth celebrating. Yesterday's 2% total revenue gain masks a retail explosion (up 99%) that offset a 4% grooming dip; the grooming softness was capacity-constrained (7 groomers vs trailing baseline productivity of $479/groomer-day, you hit $451), and bather hours ran 23% light at 4.0h vs 5.2h average. Tighten bather scheduling back to target and the grooming line recovers. Critical: restock X-Small Treat Table (out of stock) and Shanti's Medical Journey (47 units short of monthly need)\u2014retail momentum is real, don't leave money on the shelf.\n\nHicksville is the operational bright spot. Yesterday grooming revenue jumped 25% on just 3 active groomers; that's $447/groomer-day, beating the trailing 30-day baseline of $421. Month-to-date you're up 16.5% on DOW adjustment\u2014outperforming Port on a per-groomer basis. The trade-off: retail cratered 56% and transaction count dropped 14%, suggesting front-of-house focus has narrowed to grooming execution. Retail inventory gaps are deeper here (X-Small Treat Table at -144 units, Cannoli at -67); this is suppressing add-on attach (33% vs Port's 48%). Restock treats aggressively and task someone with retail suggestive selling.\n\nOne pattern: both locations are constrained by non-groomer capacity (bather hours, retail focus). Port has the groomer depth; Hicksville is proving it can run lean and hot. Neither location is a demand problem\u2014appointments are strong and productivity per groomer is solid.\n\n**Capacity & coverage: Today Port has 7 groomers + 7.0h bathers, Hicksville has 3 groomers + 0.0h bathers; tomorrow Port drops to 6 groomers + 7.0h bathers, Hicksville rises to 4 groomers + 0.0h bathers; next 7 days Port carries 38 groomer-days and Hicksville 18 groomer-days (56 total), with 